// QUEST_STORY: an optional paragraph for the first and last quest of each storyline (chains of three or more), shown
// behind "More" in the quest dialog. Spoken by the quest giver; checked by tools/lorekeeper.js at the quest's level.
(function (root) {
  const D = root.D;
  D.QUEST_STORY = {
    a_new_threat: 'I have walked this valley for forty winters, and I never saw the troggs this bold. They climb out of cracks in the stone that were not there last spring, as if something down below is pushing them up. Anvilmar is a few stone walls and a stubborn lot of dwarves. Stubborn will not be enough if they keep coming.',
    stolen_journal: 'Felix has written in that journal for years: every storm, every trogg seen, the name of every dwarf who stood a watch here. Grik\'nir did not take it to read it. He took it because he knows it matters to us. You have already thinned his whelps in that cave. Now show the old troll that this valley keeps what is its own.',
    report_kharanos: 'Senir Whitebeard has watched the Frostmane longer than any of us, and he keeps count of every camp they raise. If the trolls are pushing into our valley, they are pushing towards Kharanos as well. Tell him what you saw in the cave, all of it. The pass is cold and the wolves are hungry, so keep moving and keep your hood up.',
    frostmane_seers: 'You have seen how the trolls fight now, closer to the town every week. That is the seers\' doing. They sit by their fires and tell the Frostmane that these mountains were theirs before the first dwarf cut stone, and the young ones believe every word. Perhaps it was so, once. It is our home now, and Kharanos will not be given up to an old story.',
    road_dolanaar: 'Dolanaar sits on the road between this glade and Darnassus, and Tallonkai has watched over it since Teldrassil first took root. He is a Druid of the Claw, and patient with the young, though he would never say so. Walk softly under the great boughs. The forest is still young, and not everything that grows in it has grown as we hoped.',
    oakenscowl_q: 'I knew Oakenscowl when the Gnarlpine were our friends. He brought his people to trade at our fires and sat with our druids through the long nights. Something has rotted in him since, and in all of them. You have seen what his warriors have become. If he falls, perhaps what is left of his tribe will remember who they were. I no longer expect it.',
    gnarlpine_shamans: 'The shamans were the tribe\'s healers once. They knew every root and spring on the island, and the young furbolgs listened to them above all others. That is why the corruption spreads so fast: it speaks with the voices the Gnarlpine trust most. You have fought their warriors at Ban\'ethil. The shamans are worse, because they believe they are helping.',
    report_razor_hill: 'Gar\'Thok has held Razor Hill since we first came to this land. He is hard and he speaks little, but he does not waste warriors. The humans at Tiragarde Keep watch our coast from their walls, and Razor Hill is the first thing they see. Go to him with your head up. He will know soon enough what you are worth.',
    benedict_q: 'The insignias you brought me tell the story. These are not lost sailors. They are soldiers of Kul Tiras, and they built a keep on our shore as if the land were theirs to take. Benedict gives them their orders and their courage. Without him the keep is only stone and frightened men. The peace between our peoples is thin. He has made it thinner.',
    report_bloodhoof: 'Bloodhoof Village is the heart of our people on the plains, and Baine is the son of our high chieftain. He is young, but the Earth Mother gave him his father\'s patience. Go down the mountain slowly and look at the land as you pass. Mulgore feeds us and asks little in return. Whoever harms it answers to every tauren.',
    last_poachers: 'The Palemane do not hunt to eat. They take the hides and leave the rest for the vultures, and the herds we have followed for generations grow thinner every season. You drove them back once already, for Baine. These are the last of them, and the most stubborn. When the rock is quiet, the plainstriders will graze there again. I would like to see that before winter.',
    report_gryan: 'I have asked Stormwind for soldiers to send west. Every answer comes back the same: the army is needed elsewhere, and Westfall must look after itself. So the farmers did. Gryan Stoutmantle was a soldier once, and he has turned their pitchforks into a militia. He needs every blade he can get. I cannot spare him mine, but I can send him you.',
    wanted_highwaymen: 'You cleared the road once, and the carts are moving again. But the ones who still ride are the old hands, the ones who taught the rest to rob. I have watched them from the hills for weeks. They know every gully between here and Moonbrook, and they never camp twice in one place. When they are gone, the farmers can drive without looking over their shoulders.',
    unsent_letter_q: 'Think of what you have seen since you came to Sentinel Hill: the trappers, the pathstalkers, the orders, Moonbrook burned to its foundations. Now a sealed letter, and a ship. Stolen coin does not build a warship. Someone is paying VanCleef, and whoever it is sits a long way from Westfall. Finish him. What he carries may tell us who.',
    disrupt_attacks: 'The Crossroads feeds half the Barrens. Every caravan from Orgrimmar passes under these walls, and every one the centaurs take means a hungry outpost somewhere down the road. The Kolkar say this land is theirs by their fathers\' blood, and they mean to take it back one wagon at a time. Show them the Horde does not pay tolls.',
    kolkar_leaders: 'You have killed his drudges and his wranglers, and still Barak Kodobane sends raiders to our roads. He is no fool. He knows the Horde is stretched thin out here, and he is waiting for us to tire. Every clan of the Kolkar watches the Stagnant Oasis to see who is stronger. When he falls, they will remember what they saw.',
    lushwater_scout: 'I have hunted raptors since I was a child, and the ones at Lushwater are different. They hunt in packs, they learn where the caravans stop, and they come back to the same places. That is not hunger. That is patience. Look before we fight. I want to know how many there are, and how clever.',
    lushwater_raptors: 'Sergra tells me you have hunted the scytheclaws already. The drivers are grateful, but they still come in with torn wagons and fewer kodos than they left with. They sit at my fire and drink and do not talk. An innkeeper learns to read silence. When the oasis is clear, perhaps they will tell stories again instead of counting the dead.',
    rusty_golems: 'Those golems were built to plough and to reap. My family saved two seasons of coin to hire one, back when Westfall still had coin to save. Now they walk in circles on the Dead Acre and crush whatever is left of the crops. Salma cries when she sees them from the window. I do not want revenge. I just want my fields back.',
    dead_acre_sweep: 'Saldean told me what you did for his fields. The rusty golems were old machines gone wrong. The reapers are new, and new machines cost money the Brotherhood should not have. I cannot stop whoever is building them, not yet. I can stop the machines. When the Dead Acre is quiet, the farmers can plant again, and Westfall can eat.',
    digsite_scout: 'The dwarves say they dig for old stones and the bones of dead kings. Perhaps they do. But they brought rifles and soldiers with their shovels, and they never asked whose hills they were digging in. Ironforge and Orgrimmar are not at war. Soldiers with guns on Horde land make that hard to remember. I want to know what they are really after.',
    digsite_sweep: 'From Far Watch I can see their fires every night, closer to Thorn Hill than they were the month before. Thork sends scouts and asks questions. I send warriors. You have seen their excavators at work, tearing the hills open as if the land owed them something. The Barrens are Horde land. The dwarves can dig in their own mountains.',
    baeldun_orders_q: 'You broke their soldiers, and still the dig goes on. That means someone above them wants it very badly. If Ironforge itself sent them, this is not a band of treasure hunters on our land. It is a choice made by a king. I must see those orders with my own eyes before I send word to Orgrimmar. Words like these start wars.',
    windshear_scout: 'I have walked these mountains since I was a calf, and the earth has always spoken to me quietly. Now it screams. The goblins came with saws and fire and machines that walk like men, and they cut the forest as if it would grow back by morning. It will not. Go and see it for yourself. Then tell me I am wrong.',
    deforesters: 'You have stopped many of the loggers, and the goblins have answered the only way they know. What they cannot carry away, they burn. The smoke reaches Sun Rock at night, and the young ones cough in their sleep. Ash will lie on the crag for years, and nothing will grow there until the rains have washed it clean.',
    renders_scout: 'The Blackrock have come down from the mountains before, but never in such numbers, and never to stay. They camp under Render\'s Rock as if the valley were theirs. I have written to Stormwind twice for soldiers and heard nothing back. Until someone answers, Lakeshire defends itself. I need to know how many there are, and how well they are armed.',
    renegades: 'You have seen their outrunners on our roads. The renegades are worse. They burn barns, drive off the cattle and leave families with nothing to see the winter through. Some of those farmers held the line with me against the Horde in the Second War. They should not have to do it again with pitchforks. Stormwind has not answered. You did.',
    logging_orders_q: 'Goblins do nothing without a contract, and nothing on a contract comes cheap. Someone is paying the Venture Co. to strip these mountains bare, and paying well. I want to know who. The deforesters carry their orders in their pockets, the way they carry everything of value. Paper remembers what goblins would rather forget.',
    pump_valves: 'The orders led you to Cragpool, and you saw what they have done there. The lake was deep when my grandmother was young. Now the goblins pump it dry to feed their machines, and the streams below it run thin. Every creature in these hills drinks from that water. Let the mountain have back what is its own.',
    cragpool_patrol: 'Mastok tells me you have fought their operators at the dam. The goblins will not give up so easily. They count trees and water the way we count our ancestors, and they hate to lose a single one. If we leave the works standing, new crews will come with the next shipment. Break them so completely that the Venture Co. decides these mountains cost too much.',
    galardell_scout: 'My brother is not a liar. He drinks, and he tells tall stories about the fish he caught, but he came home white as milk and would not say a word until morning. Small black dragons, he said, no bigger than dogs, and grass burned black around them. The marshal laughed. Please, just look. If he is wrong, I will make him apologise.',
    whelp_hunt: 'Solomon has his scales, and his letters to Stormwind will be long ones. I am a soldier. I do not need proof, I need a plan. Whelps do not hatch by themselves in Galardell Valley. Something laid those eggs and is keeping them warm, and it is bigger than anything this town has ever faced. We start with what we can kill.',
    charred_vale_scout: 'The Charred Vale burned long before any of us came to Sun Rock. Some say it was a war between the old spirits of the land. Some say it was worse. Nothing tall grows there even now. The harpies like it because nobody else will go near it. I want to know how many have gathered there, and why they have grown so bold.',
    storm_witches: 'You have fought the Bloodfury on our patrol roads and seen how they hate everything that walks on the ground. The storm witches are the worst of them. When the sky is clear, they call it dark, and their lightning finds the driest trees first. The Vale burned once already. I will not watch the rest of these mountains follow it.',
    blackrock_orders_q: 'Orcs raiding farms is a misery. Orcs following written orders is a war. The Blackrock have officers now, and the officers have plans, and nobody in Stormwind reads my reports. I have been magistrate of this town for many years, and I have never been so frightened for it. I want to know what they intend before they do it.',
    summoner_tomes: 'Stonewatch was ours. Our grandfathers built it to guard these hills against the orcs, and now the orcs sit behind its walls and call fire down on us from our own towers. You have seen the keep. Their fire has already taken two barns and a family\'s home. I want to know who taught the Blackrock magic like that, and what else they learned.',
    gathilzogg_q: 'You have read their orders, walked the keep and cut down the champions on its walls. All of it leads back to one orc. Gath\'Ilzogg has burned this valley for years, and every time we drove his raiders off, he came back with more. When his head is in my hands, every orc in these hills will know Lakeshire is not theirs. Perhaps Stormwind will finally listen.',
    keep_patrol: 'Stonewatch Keep was built to guard this valley. My grandfather helped to lay its stones. Now the orcs keep their war plans behind those walls, and you have seen for yourself how many hold them. Marshal Marris says the champions you cut down were replaced within a day. So we keep them busy. An orc watching his own walls is an orc not coming down to the lake.',
    grimtotem_scout: 'The Grimtotem were our kin. They shared our fires and our songs. Then they decided the tauren should rule the land rather than listen to it, and they turned from the Earth Mother to do it. I can feel their totems in the ground from here, like a splinter under the skin. Go carefully. They will not treat you as a guest.',
    grimtotem_plans: 'Tsunaman grieves for these people. I understand it, but grief does not guard a gate. You have seen their brutes on the path, and you know they mean to come here. A warrior without a plan dies brave. A warrior who has read his enemy\'s plan goes home. The mystics carry theirs. Bring it to me, and Sun Rock will be ready for them.',
    grimtotem_mystics_q: 'The totems you brought me were carved with care. That is what troubles me most. Whoever made them knew our oldest prayers and turned each one inside out. The mystics were healers and seers once, the same as any of our elders. Now they spend their gifts on curses. I cannot break a curse faster than they carve one. It has to stop at the hand.',
    worgen_fangs: 'Brightwood Grove was where Darkshire went for timber, and for a quiet walk when the day was done. Nobody walks there now. The Nightbane came out of those trees a few years ago, and they grow bolder every season. The Night Watch is farmers and clerks with a spear each. We lose one or two a month. I would rather we lost worgen.',
    brightwood_patrol: 'Sirra has your charms on her desk and has hardly slept since. She says the weavers are not simply beasts, that someone taught them what they know. I leave the books to her. My concern is the road. The watch walks Brightwood tonight, and they will walk it better with you beside them. Keep your torch high and your back to someone you trust.',
    farmers: 'Some of those farmers were my neighbours. I buried a few of their parents myself, before the plague made burying pointless. They look at us now and see monsters. So be it. Every sack of grain from Hillsbrad Fields feeds soldiers who would burn Tarren Mill to the ground and call it a mercy. I do not need them to understand. I need the fields quiet.',
    farm_patrol: 'The High Executor sees those fields as a supply line. I see them as soil, and soil remembers. The farmhands you dealt with will be replaced and the rows replanted, unless someone keeps the pressure on. A farm that cannot bring in a harvest two seasons running is a farm that gets abandoned. The living are in a hurry. We are patient. We have to be.',
    pyrewood_scout: 'I knew Pyrewood before the plague. Fishing boats, a mill, an inn with bad ale. Arugal\'s curse did not kill the village. It did something worse. By day the people mend their nets and remember nothing. By night they hunt. I want to understand the spell before anyone tries to break it, and I cannot study it from a chair in Tarren Mill.',
    moonrage_patrol: 'The fangs you brought back told me what I feared. The curse is not fading with time. It is feeding. Every season the Moonrage hold more of the road, and the keep draws more of the forest into its shadow. If we mean to go up there, the way has to stay open. I will not send anyone to Shadowfang Keep along a road already full of wolves.',
    azurelode_ore_q: 'Azurelode ore takes an edge like nothing else in these hills. The humans pull it out of the ground and ship it away to make swords for soldiers who ride against us. That offends me twice: once as a warrior and once as a smith. Bring me their ore, and I will make something from it that the Alliance will remember.',
    mine_patrol: 'Krusk tells me the mine went quiet for three days. Three days. Then the wagons came back with new faces and new picks. The humans have more miners than sense. I have forged good steel from the ore you brought me, and every blade makes me want the rest. They will stop sending men when they stop getting them back.',
    bluegill_raiders: 'Menethil lives on the sea. Half this harbour eats what the boats bring in, and the other half drinks to it. The Bluegill know the fishing grounds as well as we do. They come up under the hulls at dusk, cut the nets and drag off whatever they can reach. Last week they took a lad off the end of the pier. We did not get him back.',
    marsh_patrol: 'Rethiel says the water is cleaner since you broke those oracles, and the boats have had two quiet nights. Two. I have been a captain long enough to know that a quiet night means they are gathering. The Bluegill never stop. They only pause. So we push them back into the reeds before they remember how many of them there are.',
    raptor_eggs: 'This is the most promising dig the Explorers\' League has had in years. There are carvings down there older than anything I have seen, and I would stake my beard on it. And the raptors have decided it is the perfect place to raise their young. I have lost two tents and very nearly a digger. Without the eggs, they have no reason to come back.',
    dig_patrol: 'After you cleared the nests we had a fortnight of real work. We found a stair going down. A proper stair, cut by hands, not by water. Then the screechers came back, and screechers bring the rest of the flock with them. I will not lose this site to a pack of overgrown chickens. Keep them off my people, and I will keep digging.',
    tranquil_scout: 'Heh. I used to walk that cemetery every evening. Lovely place. Quiet neighbours. The town keeps telling me the dead have started wandering about in there, and the town says a great many things. I would go myself, but my knees are not what they were, and I have work waiting at home. You go. Tell me what you see. Tell me everything.',
    rotted_ones: 'Commander Ebonlocke tells me the plague spreaders are fewer since you went among them. The rotted ones are another matter. They are slow, and they are many, and they are heading for the farms that feed this town. Darkshire has asked Stormwind for help every year I have been mayor. We are still asking. Until someone answers, we rely on people like you.',
    raven_hill_scout: 'Raven Hill had a village once, and a chapel, and good people who buried their dead properly. The records stop the year the darkness came to this forest. I have read about the place for years from a safe distance. Now the watch says the dead are climbing out, and I find my curiosity is stronger than my sense. Go and see for me.',
    raven_hill_patrol: 'The grave moss you brought me grows only where dark magic has soaked into the soil for years. Raven Hill is not waking by accident. The mages you cut down were keeping the dead on their feet, and something is still making more of them. I have found three Darkshire families in the old records who are buried up there. I would like them to stay buried.',
    wanted_stitches: 'Ribs, then hearts. You brought that old man everything he asked for, and the whole town let him have it, me included. None of us asked what he was building out there. Now it walks the road, and the watch has already lost good people to it. I am not blaming you. I am asking you to finish what he started.',
    durnholde_scout: 'Durnholde was a human prison once. After the Second War they kept orcs there in pens, like cattle. The walls are thick and the cells are deep, and now the Syndicate have taken it for their own. They are what is left of the nobles of Alterac: thieves with family crests. I want to know how many of them there are before I decide anything.',
    durnholde_patrol: 'You have seen what the Syndicate are now: a few nobles, a great many rogues, and mercenaries paid to do the dying. They cannot hold the road and the keep at once. So we make them choose. Every sword they keep on Durnholde\'s walls is one that is not robbing our caravans. The Dark Lady wants this land. We will take it in pieces.',
    vulgol_scout: 'The ogres came down from the hills this year. They piled up earth and timber south of the river until they had a mound you can see from the town wall, and they call it Vul\'Gol. Ogres do not build unless they mean to stay. I need to know what we are facing before the woodcutters refuse to go out at all.',
    taskmasters: 'The woodcutters went back to the river after you dealt with the Splinter Fist warriors. They were back in town by noon. The taskmasters had simply driven fresh ogres out to meet them. An ogre left to itself would rather eat and sleep than raid. It is the whip that makes them march. Take the whips away, and I think we may have some peace.',
    dun_modr_scout: 'Dun Modr was ours. My grandfather stood watch on those walls. The Dark Iron took it while our backs were turned, and they have been digging under it ever since. What for, nobody knows. The Dark Iron do not dig for gold alone. Count them, look at what they are building, and come back and tell me. Then we will talk about taking it back.',
    saboteurs: 'The Thandol Span is the only road north for an army. Lose it, and Arathi might as well be across the sea. The Dark Iron know that. Glorin says the ones you killed at Dun Modr were diggers and guards. The saboteurs are something else: trained, patient, and carrying enough powder to drop a mountain. I want that powder in my storeroom, not under my bridge.',
    modr_patrol: 'I have been working the scraps you brought me. The Dark Iron fold their steel in a way I have never seen, and I have stood at this anvil for forty years. I could learn it, given time. Time is what I need from you. Keep them behind their walls and busy, and I will keep my forge hot. Good steel is worth a few bruises.',
    orchard_scout: 'The same cards have come up three nights running. The withered tree. The wolf that stands. The moon turned upside down. I do not like it when they repeat themselves. It means they are tired of being ignored. Something waits in that orchard, and it has been waiting a long time. The cards do not say what. They only say you should be the one to see it.',
    vile_fangs: 'I have been mayor through the ogres, the ghouls and the restless dead, and still it is the worgen that keep this town awake. Commander Ebonlocke says the dark runners are fewer since you hunted them. The vile fangs are the ones the rest follow. Break them, and the pack may scatter. Darkshire has not had a quiet night in years. I would like to see one.',
    growless_scout: 'Our plagues take men, bears, horses, even rats. Not the yetis. They wander through ground we have soaked and come out whole. The Society would pay a great deal to know why, and I would pay more. Something in their fur, or their blood, or the cold of that cave. Go and look at them. Do not get eaten. I need your notes intact.',
    ferocious_yetis: 'You killed the cave yetis, and the raids slowed. Slowed, not stopped. The big ones came down themselves last night and took two carts and a wolf. A wolf! I have fought men and trolls and worse, and I have never seen anything throw a wolf. The apothecaries can keep their studies. I want the ones that lead the raids dead.',
    yeti_patrol: 'The fur you brought me has told me a great deal, and none of it pleases the Society. The plague does not fail on the yetis by chance. It finds nothing in them to hold on to. I need more time with them, and fewer of them between me and the answer. One more push. Then I can go back to my cellar and my notes.',
    angerfang_scout: 'Grim Batol is a dwarven fortress. Was, I should say. The Dragonmaw have held it since the Second War, and they keep something up there with red wings, in chains. Nobody who went to look came back to tell it properly. Their camp at the foot of the mountain is as close as I want anyone to get, for now.',
    shadowcasters: 'The grunts you killed were brave enough. Riders and axes, nothing more. The shadowcasters are the ones who keep the dragons in line, and a beast only obeys that sort of magic because it hurts. I would sooner not think about what they do to those creatures. Bring me their orders. I want to know who they answer to, and who pays for the chains.',
    raptor_mastery: 'Hemet chases the big cats. I have always preferred the raptors. They hunt in threes, they learn your habits, and they will circle round behind you while you are admiring the one in front. Lake Nazferiti is full of them. A hunter who can hold his own there has earned a place at the expedition\'s fire. Mind the reeds, and never trust a quiet shore.',
    lake_patrol: 'The talons look splendid on the wall. Hemet pretends not to be jealous. Now for the practical side of the business. The boats bring our supplies up the water, and the crews will not land while raptors and tigers own the shore. A hunter should know when sport becomes work. This is work, I am afraid, but it is still good hunting.',
    stalker_hunt: 'Erlgadin tells me you have done his whole list, lake to hides. Good. These last stalkers are the old ones, grey about the jaw, and they have outlived every hunter who came for them. They have also started taking the expedition\'s pack mules. I do not hunt for walls. I hunt so the camp eats and the mules live. Finish it.',
    bloodscalp_h: 'Long ago dis whole jungle belong to da trolls, one empire from da coast to da mountains. Now da tribes fight over da bones of it. Da Bloodscalp hunt Darkspear like game, and dey hang what dey take in da ruins of Zul\'Daia. Da Horde give us a home when nobody else would. Here, we pay dat back.',
    troll_patrol: 'Nimboya counts tusks. I count the days until the Bloodscalp stop coming. Your work has thinned them, and now they send axe throwers to the tree line to pick off my grunts from cover. This base camp was cut out of the jungle by hand, and the jungle wants it back. I will not give the trolls the chance to help it.',
    kurzen_commandos: 'We came to this jungle as Stormwind soldiers, sent to hunt trolls under Colonel Kurzen. Somewhere in those years he stopped sleeping and started talking to the trees. When he turned on his own officers, a handful of us walked out. The men you\'ll fight used to share our fires. Some of them I trained myself. Don\'t hesitate. They won\'t.',
    colonel_kurzen_q: 'Yohwa read the orders you brought back. There is no plan in them, only raids, and a list of the men he thinks betrayed him. My name is first. Stormwind will call us deserters either way; nobody at court has written to this camp in years. So we finish it ourselves. When it\'s done, I\'ll bury him properly. He was a good officer once.',
    a_thrashers: 'Refuge Pointe lives on what comes over the Thandol Span. Grain, arrows, bandages, the odd letter from home. All of it rolls across the plains in open carts, and the thrashers have learned what a cart means. They take the oxen first, then the drivers. I\'ve lost two good teamsters this month. The League can\'t hold Arathi if it can\'t feed itself.',
    a_plains_patrol: 'The carts are getting through again, and the drivers asked me to thank you. Now the fleshstalkers have moved into the ground the thrashers left, and they hunt smarter. That road is older than any of us. Arathor laid it when this was the first kingdom of men. I\'d like the League to still be using it when I\'m too old to walk it.',
    h_thrashers: 'Our wolf riders carry every message Hammerfall sends across these highlands. A wolf can outrun a human. It cannot outrun a thrasher on open ground. They come low through the grass, three or four together, and go for the wolf\'s legs. A rider left on foot out there does not live long. Thin them, and my riders can run again.',
    h_plains_patrol: 'The claws you brought are on every hunter\'s belt in Hammerfall now. The young ones wear them for luck. But the fleshstalkers were waiting behind the thrashers all along, and they are bolder. The humans at Refuge Pointe want this road as much as we do. They will not have it. Clear it once more, and it stays ours.',
    a_kobolds: 'I was born in Stromgarde, before the ogres and the Syndicate took it. My father used to say there was gold in Drywhisker Gorge, and nobody believed him. The kobolds seem to. They\'ve dug so deep the hillside above the gorge has started to slump. Whatever\'s down there, it belongs to Arathor, not to rats with candles on their heads.',
    a_gorge_patrol: 'Captain Nials sent your ore samples over the Span to Menethil to be looked at. No answer yet, and the kobolds are not waiting for one. New tunnels open every night, and the diggers are pushing west, towards the road. My father was right about the gorge, I think. I just never pictured the kobolds finding it first.',
    h_kobolds: 'The earth under Hammerfall is old, and it is patient. The kobolds are neither. They dig without asking and they dig without stopping, and I can feel the gorge ache when I put my hands to the stone. Hammerfall was built by orcs who remember the humans\' camps. We will not be driven from it by vermin with candles.',
    h_gorge_patrol: 'The shaman have held the candles and ore you brought, and listened to them. Wax, copper and fear. The kobolds do not dig for riches, whatever the humans at Refuge Pointe believe. They dig because they have always dug, and their newest tunnels run under our walls. If the earth gives way beneath Hammerfall, it will not be the earth\'s fault.',
    a_syndicate: 'Stromgarde was a great city of men when Stormwind was still a village. Its walls still stand. What lives inside them now is the Syndicate: thieves with good boots and better manners, who hire out their knives to anyone who pays. The League of Arathor was founded to take that city back, stone by stone. We start at the gates.',
    a_keep_patrol: 'We hold the outer wall now, which is more than the League has held in years. Shards walked the old market yesterday and said nothing for an hour. The Syndicate will come back; they always do, because someone keeps paying them to. So we keep knocking them down until that someone runs out of coin.',
    h_syndicate: 'The Syndicate call themselves free men. Free to rob, free to murder. They take Horde scouts on the roads and sell the scalps to Alliance bounty hunters, and they would sell Alliance heads to us if we paid. They have no side but their purse. Stromgarde\'s stones mean nothing to Hammerfall. The thieves hiding in them do.',
    h_keep_patrol: 'Gorn says the magi you killed were burning letters when you reached them. Men who burn letters are afraid of who might read them. The Dark Lady\'s people in the hills want every Syndicate name they can get, and I want every Syndicate road closed. Keep them down in their ruin. A thief who cannot leave his hole cannot sell anyone.',
    southsea_pirates: 'Gadgetzan runs on two things: water and trade. The water I can guard. The trade comes by sea, and the Southsea pirates at Lost Rigger Cove have decided every ship in these waters owes them a toll. I don\'t mind a toll. I charge plenty myself. I mind one I\'m not collecting. The hats go on my office wall. A baron needs a hobby.',
    keelhaul_q: 'Bilgewhizzle tells me the water towers haven\'t taken a single cannonball since your trip to the cove. First good news I\'ve had from that man all year. Keelhaul won\'t quit, though. He\'d sooner sink with his ship than look soft in front of his crew. Once his hook hangs next to those hats, the rest of the Southsea will find somewhere else to fish.',
    silithid_carapaces: 'I\'ve mapped every dune between here and the crater, and I\'ve never seen anything like the Noxious Lair. The mounds weren\'t on my first maps. Now they\'re taller than the water towers, and they hum at night. The insects inside aren\'t scorpids or beetles. They build like engineers and fight like soldiers. I want a carapace on my bench before I sleep again.',
    stinger_venom_q: 'The carapaces you brought are harder than any steel Blizrik sells, and lighter. That alone would be worth a fortune. What worries me is the hive. You killed dozens of them for Bilgewhizzle, and the mound is still growing. Something in there is feeding it. The venom may tell me what, or at least how fast. Handle the sacs carefully. I mean it.',
    dunemaul: 'Every caravan out of Gadgetzan pays me a fee, and I promise it arrives. The Dunemaul ogres are making a liar of me. They sit on the dunes and wait, and when a wagon comes past they eat the driver\'s lunch and, sometimes, the driver. Some of those wagons carry cargo I\'m paid not to look at. Those owners are the least patient of all.',
    dune_patrol: 'My water carts use the same road as the caravans, and the ogres don\'t care which is which. A cart of spilled water in Tanaris is a crime against nature. Blizrik has been stringing those ogre teeth you brought him into a necklace, which is disgusting. The Dunemaul are angry about it and pushing up the road again. Push back, please. Before breakfast, ideally.',
    fa_stagwings: 'Feathermoon stands on an island, and every word we send to the mainland goes by hippogryph. The Frayfeather flocks were never friendly, but they left our riders alone. This season they rise against anything with wings that is not their own. We have lost two messengers and the letters they carried. The Sentinels do not like silence from their own scouts.',
    fa_highland_patrol: 'The arrows fletched with your plumes fly truer than anything I have made in a century. I am almost sorry for the birds. Almost. The skystormers lead the flocks now, and they have learned where our riders cross the highlands. Clear the road once more. I would like my next shipment of arrows to reach the Sentinels, not the bottom of a ravine.',
    fh_stagwings: 'Camp Mojache is far from Thunder Bluff, and we live by what our scouts see. The hippogryphs of the highlands have always been restless, but this season they dive on our scouts out of the cloud, and a tauren does not fall softly. I take no joy in this hunt. The Earth Mother asks us to keep our people alive, and I intend to.',
    fh_highland_patrol: 'Orwin hung your plumes on the camp\'s totems, and the young braves touch them for luck on the way out. Good for them. I make the spears those braves carry, and I\'m tired of mending ones that came back from the highlands snapped in half. The skystormers lead the flock now. Kill enough of them and the rest will remember to keep to the sky.',
    fa_woodpaw: 'The Woodpaw gnolls have always raided, but they used to take what they could carry and run. Now they burn what they cannot carry. Traders from the coast have stopped using the hill roads, and the few who still come arrive with gnoll arrows stuck in their packs. Shandris wants the roads open before the rains. So do I.',
    fa_gnoll_patrol: 'Shandris burned the manes you brought, out where the gnolls could see the smoke. Without their mystics to call the pack, the Woodpaw are only angry, not organised. One more sweep of the hills, and they will go back to hunting deer. I have not slept a full night since spring. I would like to remember what it feels like.',
    fh_woodpaw: 'The Woodpaw live in the hills above us, and they think the forest is theirs because they were here first. Perhaps it is. They still do not get to burn our tents. Last week they took a kodo calf and two of our herbalists\' packs, and left an elder with a broken arm. Show them Camp Mojache has teeth.',
    fh_gnoll_patrol: 'The manes you brought are in the fire pit now, and the smoke smells terrible. Good. The mystics braided their charms from those, and without them the Woodpaw bicker among themselves. One more sweep and they will leave us in peace for a season. The elder with the broken arm is mending, and asked me to thank you. Finish it, and we all sleep.',
    fa_gordunni: 'Before the Sundering, Feralas was Kaldorei country, and our ruins still stand under the moss. Some of them hold things we would rather stayed buried. The Gordunni have found them. They dig with no care and no understanding, and their mage-lords have started reading what they find. An ogre with an elven scroll sounds like a joke. It is not.',
    fa_ogre_patrol_f: 'I read the scrolls you took from the mage-lords. Most were prayers, a few were poetry, one was a list of grain stores from before the Sundering. Harmless, all of them. The next ones may not be. Our ancestors wrote things in those halls that should never have been written down, and the ogres cannot tell the difference. Keep them busy, and away from the deeper ruins.',
    fh_gordunni: 'The Gordunni hold the outpost and the old roads around it. They are not stupid, whatever the young braves say. They set ambushes where the trees close in, and they carry off our wounded instead of finishing them. We have not learned what they do with them. I would rather you thin their numbers than we find out.',
    fh_ogre_patrol_f: 'Orwin says the scrolls you took are night elf writing, older than anything in Thunder Bluff. The ogres cannot read them, but their mage-lords are trying, and that is enough to frighten me. We have pushed them back from our paths. One of our wounded crawled home, thin but alive. Keep the ogres busy. Every day they fight us is a day they are not digging.',
    fa_hatecrest: 'The naga were Kaldorei once, the proudest of us, before the Well of Eternity took their city into the sea. Whatever the deep made of them, they remember this coast and want it back. The Hatecrest come out of the surf at night and strike our watchposts. I have buried enough of my Sentinels this year. I do not intend to bury more.',
    fa_coast_patrol: 'The sirens are quiet since you went among them. Our sailors say the sea sounds empty without the singing, and some of them miss it, which tells you how dangerous it was. Shandris has walked the sea wall every night since the first raid. Hold the coast once more and she might rest. The naga will come back. They always do. But they will come back fewer.',
    fh_hatecrest: 'Tauren are not a sea people. We did not know what the naga were until they came up the beach and dragged two of our fishers into the water. The night elves say the naga were their kin, long ago. I do not care what they were. I care that they have made the coast a graveyard, and that they are moving inland.',
    fh_coast_patrol: 'The corals you brought hum when the tide turns. I keep them wrapped in hide, far from my tent. The sirens are fewer now, and our fishers have gone back to the shallows, though they keep one ear on the water. Hadoken wants the beach held until the naga give it up. Once more, then. I was born on the plains. I do not trust the sea.',
    ug_bloodpetal: 'I found this crater by falling into it, more or less, and I\'ve never wanted to leave. Everything here is older than it should be. The bloodpetals by the hot springs are a good example. They move, they bite, and they drink the steam. I want sprouts to study, young ones, before they grow teeth. Well. Bigger teeth.',
    ug_springs_patrol: 'Williden\'s got your sprouts in jars on his table, and he talks to them at night. Yeager\'s got his horns. Me, I just want a bath. The Golakka springs are the only hot water in this crater that doesn\'t come out of a volcano, and the lashers and thunderers have claimed it. We came here to study this place, not to be eaten by it.',
    ug_gorishi: 'I came to this crater to study the Silithid, and I have had rather more study than I wanted. The Gorishi hive in the Slithering Scar grows every season. The wasps carry glands that tell the swarm where to go. If I can learn to read that scent, perhaps I can learn to keep them away from our tents.',
    ug_hive_patrol: 'Williden has his glands and Larion has his count of reavers, and the Scar is as loud as ever. I built this refuge for explorers, not soldiers. The hive does not care what we came for. Every tunnel they dig brings them a little closer to the camp. Go back in and teach them that this ground is not theirs.',
    ug_fire_plume: 'Fire Plume Ridge is the crater\'s chimney. When the mountain breathes out, the fire gets up and walks. I want those embers to learn what keeps an elemental burning so far from any other fire. Also, my kettle has been cold for a week. Science first, tea second. Probably.',
    ug_ridge_patrol: 'The beasts will not go near the ridge now. The stompers have left their old trails and the birds nest on the far side of the crater. Fire is not meant to walk, and the whole jungle feels it when it does. You have thinned it once. Thin it again, and maybe the animals come home before the jungle forgets they lived there.',
    ug_gorillas: 'The apes in the marshes are clever. They watch the refuge from the treeline, and they have started taking our traps apart to see how they work. I respect that. I would rather leave them be. But they have hurt two of our people this month, and a beast that learns to hunt people does not unlearn it.',
    ug_marsh_patrol: 'I lost a boot, a notebook and most of my dignity in those marshes. The gorillas took the notebook. The trappers took the boot. I will not say who took the dignity. Larion says the marsh has been quieter since you went in, and quiet is good. Quiet means I can go back for my notes.',
    bsa_firegut: 'The Firegut held Dreadmaul Rock long before Stormwind raised this vigil. They raid our supply carts and eat what they catch, mules and drivers alike. I cannot watch the mountain and guard my own road with the soldiers I have. Clear the rock, and my people can turn their eyes back to where the real enemy sits.',
    bsa_ogre_patrol_bs: 'I came to the steppes to map the Ruins of Thaurissan. So far I have mapped Dreadmaul Rock instead, from behind a boulder, three times. The ogres have a gift for finding anyone holding a quill. You have done well with the brutes. One more push and I may reach the ruins with all my pages still attached to me.',
    bsh_firegut: 'The Warchief sent us here to watch the mountain, not to fight ogres. The Firegut do not know the difference. They come down from Dreadmaul Rock at dusk and take any scout who strays too far from the fire. I have lost two good runners this month. Go and remind the ogres that Flame Crest bites back.',
    bsh_ogre_patrol_bs: 'Thal\'kaur says the brutes are thinner on the ground since you went up there. Good. The Firegut carry fine iron and treat it like firewood, and they still raid the path our supplies come down. I am a smith, not a scout. I would like to spend my days at the anvil, not hiding behind it.',
    bsa_blackrock: 'The Blackrock hold their stronghold in Victor Nefarius\'s name, and they hold it well. Marshal Windsor rode past those walls into the mountain. The court calls him a deserter. I served with him, and I know better. He is down there somewhere, in the Dark Iron cells. Every battlemaster we break is one less blade between us and that door.',
    bsa_stronghold_patrol: 'I read the flamecallers\' tomes before I sent them to Stormwind. Orders, rites, and a fair drawing of this camp. The orcs are not raiding at random. Someone in the spire is planning, and planning well. I cannot storm Blackrock Mountain with what I have. What I can do is make sure whoever sits up there knows Morgan\'s Vigil is not going anywhere.',
    bsh_blackrock: 'When the Warchief led our people out of the camps, most of the clans came home. The Blackrock did not. They kept the old hunger, and now they sell their axes to Nefarius in the spire. They still wear the colours my grandfather marched beside. That is why this is a Warsong scout\'s work, and not a stranger\'s.',
    bsh_stronghold_patrol: 'The tomes you brought back are full of old shaman words turned inside out. Fire called without asking, spirits bound without leave. The Blackrock were Horde once, the same as us. They chose a master over the elements, and a mountain over their own people. Hit the stronghold again. Let Nefarius learn what the Horde does with those who betray it.',
    wpa_plaguehounds: 'Felstone Field was a farm before the plague. The hounds that run there now were somebody\'s dogs once, and the sickness has left nothing of that in them. They hunt in packs and carry the plague in every bite. Chillwind Camp cannot hold the road if the fields at our backs belong to the dead.',
    wpa_field_patrol: 'Pureheart tells me your name sits high in the Argent Dawn\'s count. Good. I would rather have numbers than prayers. These fields fed half of Lordaeron once. Nobody will plant them this year, or the next. But one day someone will, and when they do, I want there to be clean ground left for them to plant.',
    wph_plaguehounds: 'Some of us at the Bulwark kept farms in these fields, before we died. We do not speak of it much. The hounds that run Felstone Field now belong to the Scourge, and the Scourge made us what we are. The Dark Lady wants these lands back. So do I, for reasons of my own. Start with the dogs.',
    wph_field_patrol: 'Garush keeps his count for the Argent Dawn, and I let him. The living like their numbers. I notice the quiet instead. There are fewer howls at night since you went out there. The fields will stink of the plague for years yet. But they are becoming ours again, one dead thing at a time.',
    wpa_executioners: 'Andorhal was a town of granaries, the heart of this farmland. When the plague came, the grain carried it out along every road, and now the town belongs to the dead. The executioners guard its road as if it were still worth guarding. Perhaps it is. Whatever the Scourge keep in Andorhal, they do not want us near it.',
    wph_executioners: 'I knew Andorhal when it was a market town full of grain carts. Now it is full of skeletons in executioners\' hoods, marching a road nobody living uses. The Scourge guard it as though they still have something to hide there. The Forsaken remember what the Scourge did to us. Walk that road, and put them back in the ground.',
    wpa_behemoths: 'You have seen Andorhal now, so you know what the Scourge build from our dead. The behemoths in the Writhing Haunt are the worst of it, stitched together from more bodies than I care to count. Their bile is the plague at its rawest. If I can learn how it works, I may learn how to stop it before it reaches another town.',
    wph_behemoths: 'You walked through Andorhal and came back in one piece. Remarkable. The behemoths in the Haunt are the Scourge\'s finest work, if one can call it work. Their bile holds the plague at full strength. The Society wants to understand it better than the Scourge themselves do. What we do with that understanding is, I am afraid, above your rank and mine.',
    wpa_andorhal_patrol: 'The Argent Dawn does not care whose banner you carry, only that the dead stay dead. You have seen Andorhal\'s warders, and the ruins they keep. The town cannot be taken back yet. But it can be held down. Every night the Scourge spend fighting for Andorhal is a night they are not marching on Chillwind Camp.',
    wph_andorhal_patrol: 'People ask why an orc wears the colours of the Argent Dawn. I tell them the plague does not ask which side you fought on. I learned that in Lordaeron, watching it take friends of mine who never lifted a blade. The Dawn counts every kill, for every banner, and you have filled a good page of mine. Andorhal will not fall today. Help me keep it pinned.',
    ws_ursa: 'The Winterfall were never friendly, but they were never mad. They traded furs at Everlook, kept to their village and fought only when pushed. Now they drink a brew that burns the sense out of them, and they attack anything that moves. The Cenarion Circle does not kill furbolgs lightly. We do it now because nothing else is left to try.',
    ws_village: 'Their spirit beads told me what I feared. The taint did not start here. It comes out of the sick forests to the south, and the shamans have been brewing it into their drink, thinking it made them strong. Perhaps it did, for a while. Clear the village. Then I will do what I can for whatever Winterfall still have their minds.',
    ws_highborne: 'My flight remembers the night the Well of Eternity exploded. The Highborne of Kel\'Theril were proud and clever, and they died in the middle of their spells. Their ghosts are still trying to finish them. They do not know how long it has been. Releasing them is a mercy, and a duty. Their magic should not wander the snow alone.',
    ws_scales: 'Haleh gives me a look every time I mention scale, as if I were asking for her own. I am not. The scalebanes turned on their Aspect, you stopped them, and they have no more use for their hides. A smith does not waste good metal, and dragon scale is better than any metal I know. I will make something worthy of it.',
    ws_mazthoril_patrol: 'You have released the ghosts of Kel\'Theril and stopped the kin of mine who turned in Mazthoril. I still do not know who taught them to turn, and that frightens me more than anything in these caves. For now it is enough that the halls go quiet. My flight has a long memory. We will remember who stood with us when it was hard.',
    tw_husks: 'The oldest of my people still speak of the Sundering. For the husks in these orchards, it was the last thing they saw. They still tend the trees, after a fashion, walking the same rows they walked ten thousand years ago. Something under the sea keeps them moving. It is not kindness, and it was never their choice.',
    tw_sorceresses: 'The relics you brought back carry the same mark again and again: a crown above breaking waves. The Tidecrown line ruled here, and their scribes wrote everything down. These tomes are dry because the water was told not to touch them, and I think the hand that told it is still writing. That is the magic I came here to understand, and the magic I fear most.',
    tw_sentinels: 'I have sailed these waters for thirty years, and there was never land here. Now there is an island, and soldiers walking out of the surf as if they had only stepped away for a moment. My scouts say they did not shout and did not bleed. A Kul Tiran does not give ground to anyone. Not even to the drowned.',
    tc_deepmother_a: 'When the crown came off, I hoped the drowned would lie down. They did not. Aeldran wore it for ten thousand years and never understood what it was. The Deepmother kept his court alive because it served her, the way a net serves the one who casts it. The Horde are going down after her too. For once, I do not mind sharing the work.',
    sr_wavebreakers: 'The Darkspear have a song about a tribe that lived on the reefs and fished the deep water, until the sea took them all in one night. We thought it was only a song. Now the Wavebreakers walk the shallows with coral in their hair, praying to their loa. They are our blood, far back. You do not leave family walking.',
    tc_deepmother_h: 'Mazu\'s grandmother kept a shell that sang when you held it to your ear. She said it was Shal\'zua, calling the fish home. The crown is gone and the Wavebreakers sleep, but the loa does not. The thing under the citadel wears her face and sings with a stranger\'s mouth. Bring Mazu its heart, and maybe the shell sings true again.',
  };
})(typeof window !== 'undefined' ? window : globalThis);
